Shuffling
First to begin our investigation of how the correlation changes as we shuffle the data points, we have to create functions that we can iterate to give us the shuffle data frame we want.
The first function that I have created is called shuffle points. The shuffle points function intends to take in a data frame and then shuffle the tmax temperatures from that dataset. The function will return the data frame with a new column called shuffle_temp with the shuffled values.
The second function I have created is to help visualize the different correlations of these shuffle data points. The shuffle correlation function takes in a data frame and samples the maximum temperature again (we have to be careful to set the seed before calling each function so that it maintains the order), and then calculates the correlation for each shuffled temperature and subsequent rain value.


Code
shuffle_points &lt;- function(num, dataframe) {
  dataframe |&gt;
    mutate(shuffle_temp = sample(tmax))
}

shuffle_correlation &lt;- function(num, dataframe){
  dataframe |&gt;
    mutate(shuffle_tmax = sample(tmax))|&gt;
    mutate(correlation = cor(shuffle_tmax, rain))
}

Hypothesis Testing
To begin our investigation of the research question, we need to test the null hypothesis. In this scenario, we are testing whether or not the rainfall and maximum temperature happen just by random chance or if they are correlated.


Code
shuffle_points &lt;- function(num, dataframe) {
  dataframe |&gt;
    mutate(shuffle_temp = sample(tmax))
}

shuffle_correlation &lt;- function(num, dataframe){
  dataframe |&gt;
    mutate(shuffle_tmax = sample(tmax))|&gt;
    summarize(correlation = cor(shuffle_tmax, rain))
}


The first function, shuffle_points() takes in a data frame and then shuffles the tmax temperatures from that dataset. The function will return the data frame with a new column called shuffle_temp with the shuffled values.
The second function, shuffle_correlation(), takes in a data frame, randomly shuffles the maximum temperatures, and calculates one correlation between the shuffled temperatures and the rainfall. I set a seed before each simulation of both functions so the results are the same every time the code is run.
